{"h": {"measure": "A", "k": 100, "control": "D0"}, "sp": "nb", "rep": 139, "hyps": [{"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "With edge 4 absent, the 0->3 edge is one of fewer remaining inputs to the output node, so removing it is more damaging."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "direction": "higher", "why": "Removing a parameter-free pooling shortcut costs little or helps, whereas removing a conv on edge 3 loses capacity."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Adding a conv where no edge existed opens a new computational path from node 2 to the output, giving a larger gain than swapping an existing op."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "direction": "higher", "why": "Replacing avg pooling on an output edge with a 3x3 conv is among the largest single-op improvements in NAS-Bench-201."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Adding a residual input-to-output shortcut where none existed greatly eases optimization of the stacked network."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "lower", "why": "Turning a conv into a skip on the direct path sacrifices learnable capacity, so the gain is smaller or negative."}, {"edit_edge": 0, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 0, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "When edge 0 is none, node 1 is dead, so adding a conv revives edges 2 and 4 as well."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "lower", "why": "Replacing a learnable 3x3 conv with pooling on an output edge removes capacity and hurts accuracy."}, {"edit_edge": 1, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 0, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower